# Credential custody -- only a deployment that OTHER PEOPLE connect their ESPN
# accounts to needs these. A single-user local setup needs none of them:
# nothing is stored until a request carries an `espn_s2`, and your own
# bookmarklet does not send one.
#
# Nothing loads this file for you. It is a list of what to export, or what to
# give your process manager. See the README section "Holding other people's
# ESPN sessions", and pipeline/credentials.py for why each one exists.
#
# CAUTION: Keep the real key out of this repository and out of the database.
# The one property this subsystem exists to give you -- a stolen database file
# is inert -- holds only while the key is somewhere the database is not.

# REQUIRED. Generate with:
#   .venv/bin/python -c "from pipeline.credentials import generate_key; print(generate_key())"
# One key is enough. To rotate, list both: "1:<old key>,2:<new key>". New rows
# use the highest version; old rows keep working and move over as their owners
# reconnect. Versions start at 1, and one version is one key -- listing a
# version twice is refused, because the second would make the first's rows
# unreadable.
# If this is unset the server answers 503 to every request carrying a
# credential. It never falls back to storing anything in plaintext.
ESPN_CUSTODY_KEYS=

# Where the two tables live. Its own directory, created 0700, and its own file
# rather than data/nfl.duckdb -- that one gets copied to seed per-league
# databases, and stored account sessions must not ride along.
# ESPN_CUSTODY_DB_PATH=data/custody/custody.duckdb

# Idle days before a stored session is deleted. Sliding: every use pushes it
# out again. This clock is the only thing that ever removes the credential of
# a user who clears their cookies, because there is no way to reach them.
# ESPN_CUSTODY_TTL_DAYS=30

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Allows credentials over plain HTTP.
# Production leaves this unset. With it set, the session cookie also loses its
# Secure flag -- deliberately, because a Secure cookie on http is silently
# dropped by the browser and the user just looks logged out.
# Accepts 1, true, yes, on. Every other value, including 0 and false, leaves
# the protection ON.
# ESPN_CUSTODY_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_HTTP=

# Set ONLY if a proxy you control ends TLS and overwrites X-Forwarded-Proto.
# The header is client-supplied, so trusting it without that guarantee lets
# anyone skip the plain-HTTP refusal by adding one line to their request.
# Same accepted values as above.
# ESPN_CUSTODY_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO=

# -- shared storage ----------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase Postgres, session pooler. This is what lets more than one process
# hold the mutable per-user state: credential custody, Stripe entitlements and
# the live-draft session record all move here when it is set. Absent -- a
# laptop, the test suite -- every store uses its DuckDB file, which is right
# for one process and unusable for two (DuckDB locks the file for one writer).
#
# EITHER THIS ONE, WRITTEN OUT IN FULL:
# postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
# Only a postgresql:// or postgres:// value is used. Anything else -- the
# https://<ref>.supabase.co project URL, which is what the Supabase dashboard
# shows first -- is ignored with one line in the log rather than handed to the
# driver, where it would fail every custody, billing and session call.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# OR THESE TWO, WHICH ARE THE VALUES THE DASHBOARD ACTUALLY GIVES YOU: the
# project URL and the database password (shown once, when the project is
# made). Set both and the DSN above is composed from them. The password is
# URL-encoded on the way in, so punctuation in a generated one is fine, and
# it is never written to a log.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=

# The pooler's region for that composition. Defaults to us-west-2; it cannot
# be worked out from the project URL, which resolves the same wherever the
# database sits. The connection string on the dashboard has it in the host.
# SUPABASE_DB_REGION=us-west-2
